<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gustáv Husák</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gustáv Husák was a Slovak communist who was imprisoned in the 1950s but rehabilitated in the 1960s. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> invasion he replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a> as party leader in 1969 and presided over the period of normalisation, with purges and censorship. He was also president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> from 1975 until he resigned in December 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustav-husak.jpg" alt="Gustáv Husák" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kalendarakcii.bratislava.sk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gust%C3%A1v_Hus%C3%A1k_-_o%C5%99%C3%ADznuto.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gustáv Husák was born Augustin Husák on 10 January 1913 at Pozsonyhidegkút in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, now Bratislava-Dúbravka. His father, a quarryman and farmer, had been disabled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, and his mother died of tuberculosis when he was one year old. He studied at boarding schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, worked odd jobs to help his family and joined the Communist Youth Union in 1929. He began law studies at Comenius University in Bratislava in 1933 and joined the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the government of Jozef Tiso jailed him several times, and he was a leader of the Slovak National Uprising of 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1946 to 1950 Husák headed the devolved administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. He contributed strongly to the suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> of Slovakia, which had won 62 per cent in Slovakia in 1946. In 1950 he fell victim to a Stalinist purge, was arrested in 1951 and was sentenced in 1954 to life imprisonment, serving in Leopoldov Prison until 1960. His conviction was overturned in 1963. In April 1968 he became a vice-premier under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a>.</p><p>After the invasion of August 1968 he took part in the negotiations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev">Leonid Brezhnev</a> and became a leader of those who wanted the reforms reversed. He succeeded Dubček as head of the party in April 1969, purged liberal members in 1969-1971 and was elected president in 1975. The secret police ran a campaign of repression against dissidents, later represented by Charter 77. In December 1987 he gave up the party leadership to Miloš Jakeš.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 24 November 1989, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution</a>, the entire Presidium of the Communist Party, Husák included, resigned. On 10 December he swore in a government with a non-communist majority and resigned the same day. The party expelled him in February 1990, and he died on 18 November 1991, aged 78. His moral responsibility for the last two decades of communist rule remains in question. He later said he had tried to lessen the effects of the Soviet invasion and resist hardliners, while critics note that he was nominal head of the repressive apparatus and gave almost no attention to appeals from the persecuted.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gustáv Husák” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the crushing of the Prague Spring, <b>Gustáv Husák</b> presided over two decades of 'normalisation', with purges, censorship and economic stagnation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Television brought meetings between writers, political victims such as Smrkovský, Zdeněk Hejzlar and <b>Gustáv Husák</b>, and young workers and students, which moved the political dialogue from the intellectuals to the popular sphere.</blockquote><a href="/movement/prague-spring">Prague Spring · People</a></li><li><blockquote>On 10 December <b>Gustáv Husák</b> swore in the first government in 41 years not dominated by the Communists, and resigned.</blockquote><a href="/movement/velvet-revolution">Velvet Revolution · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dúbravka, in what is now <a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.19,17.04&amp;year=1963" aria-label="Dúbravka on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4946 478 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gustáv Husák"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5396 748h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5396 748h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.19,17.04&amp;year=1963">What was here in 1963? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
